<html xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word"><head><meta charset=utf-8><title>Paul Banyard - Full Pack Part 5</title></head><body><h1>Paul Banyard - Full Pack Part 5</h1><p>Paul Banyard — Full Notes &amp; Transcripts (Part 5)</p><p>Compiled from Plaud. Includes meeting notes and full transcripts. Being looked at and reviewed.</p><p>05-20 Strategic Planning</p><p>Plaud file id: b99c2409225304c83c1dd09b1b7b57e4</p><p>Notes</p><p>### Summary</p><p># Performance Review and Strategic Planning for Paul Banyard</p><p>A performance review and forward plan focused on Paul Banyard’s production results, the firm’s growth expectations, and practical steps to raise lead volume and conversion. The manager introduced a structured performance-tracking approach (written vs banked outcomes, purchase delivery, and quarterly roll-ups), reinforced a company target to improve conversion, and outlined process improvements (follow-up discipline, multi-channel contact, pipeline hygiene, and internal referrals). Paul raised substantive grievances about pay disparity and the impact of rising targets on his bonus eligibility, alongside concerns about lead quality and capacity pressures. A short operational walkthrough covered using the new platform (Plut) alongside the legacy system (Midas), including lead-source attribution and client-stage management.</p><p>---</p><p>## Retrospective Performance and Target Setting</p><p>### Historical output and current position (Paul’s stated/recorded metrics)</p><p>- Paul’s lead and case picture (as stated in the discussion): **406 leads**, **21% conversion**, **62 mortgage applications**, **34 renewal cases**, **40 insurances**, and **91 admin**.</p><p>- Fee and protection averages discussed:</p><p>  - Average admin fee (stated): **761**</p><p>  - Average renewals (stated): **724**</p><p>  - Average protection (stated): **133**</p><p>  - Another admin fee average was also stated as **249** (not reconciled in-meeting; recorded as said).</p><p>- A conversion comparison was discussed in context of case type:</p><p>  - Remortgage conversion described as potentially lower because remortgages may be **“one in four”**.</p><p>  - Purchase expectation described as closer to **“one in three”**.</p><p>- Performance-to-target context:</p><p>  - **145,000** revenue has been done (to date, per the manager).</p><p>  - A spreadsheet target of **170,000** was referenced.</p><p>  - Paul is **just shy of 50,000** already in the bank towards a **180,000** target.</p><p>  - **Q1 so far: 31,000** “in the pot”.</p><p>- Protection performance was contrasted year-on-year:</p><p>  - Protection average in **2025: 1169**</p><p>  - Protection average this year: **1882**</p><p>- Additional year-to-date averages (purchase and remortgage combined) were stated:</p><p>  - Average premium: **8893**</p><p>  - Average protection case: **182**</p><p>  - Average proc fee: **607**</p><p>  - Average case: **183**</p><p>### Targets, tracking, and reporting approach</p><p>- The meeting reiterated that this year requires “tangible changes” and clearer month-by-month tracking.</p><p>- A **performance form** will be used to track:</p><p>  - What has been **written**</p><p>  - What has been **banked**</p><p>  - How many **purchase cases** have been **actioned and delivered**</p><p>  - The form will also populate **Q1, Q2** etc., and include **remortgage opportunities** to show total annual opportunity volume.</p><p>- The company aim discussed for conversion: **60% conversion** (explicitly stated as a company aim), with a plan to track performance through the year to see whether the target is being met and why not.</p><p>- A report built by **Graham** was referenced as tracking **lead inflow**, what happens to leads, and where they go.</p><p>### Lead volume and diary expectations (team-wide framing, applied to Paul)</p><p>- The stated operational ambition: **40 appointments** in the diary **every single month**.</p><p>- The goal described: **40 leads** resulting in speaking to **30 customers**.</p><p>- The industry and company benchmark discussed:</p><p>  - Industry average conversion: **one in three**</p><p>  - The company average last year across the board: **one in three**</p><p>- “If you can get **14 new leads a month** every month, you will exceed the target” (manager’s viewpoint).</p><p>- Remortgage pipeline sizing was explicitly stated:</p><p>  - Paul has **57** potential remortgage opportunities/cases this year.</p><p>  - The company has **320** clients coming up for remortgage this year.</p><p>### Paul’s concerns about the target shift and current run-rate</p><p>- Paul stated his current bank target is **18,750** before earning **20%** bonus, and it is increasing to **21,750** (an increase of **3,000**).</p><p>- Paul described the impact as **“600 pounds coming off”** his bonus before he starts earning anything (calculated as **20% of 3,000**).</p><p>- Paul stated he currently has **6 or 7** monthly written cases, with **two more weeks** to push to **17**.</p><p>---</p><p>## Strategic Growth and Process Optimisation</p><p>### Growth strategy: more opportunities, then disciplined conversion</p><p>- The manager positioned the core growth lever as increasing opportunity flow and tightening execution:</p><p>  - Drive enough volume to speak to **30** out of **40** leads.</p><p>  - Convert at **one in three** to yield **10 new cases a month** (explicitly stated as “new cases”).</p><p>- Client and case-type positioning:</p><p>  - Average client income described as around **£100k**.</p><p>  - The firm is framed as serving **professional customers** (not “the low end”) and aiming to drive more **300,000**, **400,000**, and **500,000** mortgage cases.</p><p>### Follow-up discipline, pipeline management, and communication channels</p><p>- The manager emphasised making sure opportunities do not “slip through the gaps” by strengthening:</p><p>  - Lead management from first contact through to completion</p><p>  - Follow-up process quality</p><p>  - Maintaining an up-to-date, actively worked pipeline</p><p>- Multi-channel contact expectations were reinforced:</p><p>  - Use **text**, **email**, and **calls**, with the observation that clients are more likely to respond to **text**.</p><p>  - An example was given of setting up shorthand texting (e.g., “three letters to mean a sentence”) for speed.</p><p>### Handling different opportunity types and capacity constraints</p><p>- The team discussed using fees and delegation to protect adviser time:</p><p>  - For lower-yield cases, either complete them and **charge fees**, or pass them to junior staff (example: **Sean**) to build experience and pipeline.</p><p>- A tactical recommendation: where possible, recommend customers **tie in rates now**, then **revisit in three months**.</p><p>### Internal referrals (Neighbourhoods) as an additional income and lead channel</p><p>- Internal referral process described via **Neighbourhoods**:</p><p>  - Use **“start referral”**, follow **sales**, then select department (e.g., **Sales** or **LTOs**).</p><p>  - For area-specific referrals, check the branch covers the area before formally referring.</p><p>  - The bonus is paid **as soon as the company gets paid** (immediate linkage to receipt of company revenue).</p><p>- A specific email example was provided for a sales referral handover:</p><p>  - “Hi Miss Spaniard, get you chatting always. Copying in Priya here and she’s going to be in touch with you to raise that market appraisal.”</p><p>---</p><p>## Employee Compensation, Grievances, and Business Context</p><p>### Paul’s grievances and perceived inequity</p><p>- Paul stated:</p><p>  - His **basic salary is lower** than **Claire** and **Karen**.</p><p>  - He has **not had a pay rise for three years**.</p><p>  - The original justification given historically was **lack of experience**, but Paul now believes he has comparable experience and output.</p><p>  - He feels he contributes beyond core production (e.g., **training staff** and extracurricular contributions for Rettie) without this being reflected in compensation.</p><p>  - He believes his lead conversion is **as good as, if not better** than Claire and Karen’s, but he receives **lower quality leads**.</p><p>  - Inflation being around **3%** makes his stagnant salary feel like a **pay cut**.</p><p>  - He fears that even delivering **178k** may still leave him behind peers delivering **200k**, without achieving pay parity.</p><p>  - He described the situation as “very frustrating”, and noted personal constraints (“needs to pay a mortgage”).</p><p>### Business financial context provided by the manager</p><p>- The manager stated the business has turned a profit of only **10,000** (margin) this year, and the executive view is that prior years’ losses limit appetite to increase pay for existing staff.</p><p>- A business aspiration was framed around banking **1,000,000**, which would create a profit margin of **200,000** to **250,000**.</p><p>- The manager described the firm as being roughly **“a million quid about six hundred and fifty ish or three hundred and fifty short”** of where it needs to be minimum (as spoken; the exact shortfall figure was not clarified in the conversation).</p><p>- Paul referenced a comparator:</p><p>  - **Maz** previously on **27,500** (a couple of years ago), increasing to **30,000** by **May** last year.</p><p>  - Paul suspects Maz may rise to **32,000** or **33,000** by **May 2026** (Paul’s speculation recorded as his viewpoint).</p><p>  - Paul acknowledged Maz is in a well-established sales team that generates strong profit.</p><p>### Total Rewards Statement (new HR visibility tool)</p><p>- A new **Total Rewards Statement** was introduced as coming onto **Personio**, intended to show staff the full value of their package and earnings.</p><p>- It will include: **salary**, **bonuses**, **incentives**, **commissions**, **pension contributions**, **benefits/healthcare**, and any **referrals earned**.</p><p>- The manager described it as aligned with industry practice (e.g., annual statements received at **HSBC**).</p><p>---</p><p>## System Navigation and Operational Guidance</p><p>### Working across two systems (transition risk)</p><p>- Paul stated this year will be challenging because advisers are working across **two systems**:</p><p>  - **Midas** for legacy customers</p><p>  - **Plut** for new business</p><p>- Paul noted that occasionally staff put leads on **Midas** instead of **Plut**.</p><p>- The manager indicated an intention to pull away from Midas so clients can be managed on a single platform; however, the team acknowledged they cannot fully control the transition journey and must adapt.</p><p>### Plut platform guidance: client stages, touchpoints, and lead-source attribution</p><p>- The platform structure was described as having:</p><p>  - An **active client** section (e.g., viewing houses, submitting offers, downloading reports)</p><p>  - An **early looking customer** section (e.g., thinking of buying in **3–6 months**)</p><p>  - Customers can move between these categories.</p><p>- The manager emphasised the need to log touchpoints/communication on the platform.</p><p>- A practical issue was raised when adding a lead source:</p><p>  - The lead source dropdown did not allow directly entering the user’s name in the way expected, creating concern about accurately marking a lead as “self-generated by Paul Banyard”.</p><p>  - In practice, the system appeared to recognise **Paul Banyard** as the introducer adviser when he added it to the platform, even without explicitly selecting his name.</p><p>  - A workaround was discussed: in lead source context (e.g., under “moving”), add a note such as “change of client, call the right advice name Paul Banyard” when transferring to **Nigel**.</p><p>### Other operational notes</p><p>- It was stated that property details can be added into items on the **Barclays portal**.</p><p>- It was stated that the **FSM box** and **adding leads** would be going back to **Anna**.</p><p>---</p><p>## Action Items</p><p>### **@Speaker 2 (Manager)**</p><p>- [ ] Provide and implement the performance tracking form for month-by-month completion and weekly pre-check-in updates (tracking written, banked, and purchase cases actioned/delivered) - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Track conversion rate through the year against the stated **60%** aim using the agreed reporting approach - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Add a comment to the final section/piece of the performance report (as referenced in the meeting) - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Confirm whether the speaker needs to join another call (possibly with **McLaren**) - [TBD]</p><p>### **@Paul Banyard**</p><p>- [ ] Complete the performance tracking form **month by month** and update it **weekly prior to check-ins** (written, banked, and purchase cases actioned/delivered) - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Submit internal referral opportunities via the **Neighbourhoods** referral system (using “start referral” and selecting the correct department) - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] For a client selling a property, send the referral email introducing **Priya** to arrange a market appraisal (using the provided wording) - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Ensure purchase and remortgage client journeys are managed end-to-end so opportunities do not slip through gaps - [TBD]</p><p>### **@Anna**</p><p>- [ ] Take back ownership of the **FSM box** and the process of **adding leads** (as stated in the meeting) - [TBD]</p><p>### ADVISER WEEKLY PERFORMANCE &amp; ACTION REVIEW</p><p># Weekly Performance Review and Agreed Actions</p><p>**Adviser:** Paul Banyard  </p><p>**Reviewer:** Bob  </p><p>**Review date:** 20 May 2026  </p><p>**Content creation date:** 20 May 2026, 11:46:59  </p><p>---</p><p>## 1. Follow-Up From Previous Meeting</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>No formal action-by-action review from the previous weekly meeting was completed during the session.</p><p>However, prior planning and behavioural expectations were revisited, including:</p><p>- Paul’s annual targets.</p><p>- Lead handling and lead conversion.</p><p>- Pipeline management.</p><p>- Written and banked business tracking.</p><p>- Referral asking.</p><p>- Sales team engagement.</p><p>- Tangible behavioural changes required to increase output this year.</p><p>One previously agreed activity was specifically discussed: maintaining regular engagement with the sales teams. This has not been maintained consistently.</p><p>### Actions Previously Agreed</p><p>| Previously Agreed Action | Status | Owner | Risk Created |</p><p>|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Complete the target and reflection form, including what worked well, what did not work well, challenges faced and what will be done differently this year. | Outstanding / not confirmed as complete | Paul Banyard | Targets may remain theoretical without clear behavioural change. |</p><p>| Fill in written and banked business tracker weekly before check-ins. | Outstanding / not confirmed as complete | Paul Banyard | Management will not have an accurate view of progress against target. |</p><p>| Re-establish / maintain regular meetings with sales teams. | Outstanding / inconsistent | Paul Banyard | Lead generation remains inconsistent and adviser visibility with introducers does not improve. |</p><p>### Completed vs Outstanding</p><p>**Completed**</p><p>- No specific previous actions were confirmed as completed.</p><p>**Outstanding**</p><p>- Target and reflection form.</p><p>- Weekly written and banked business tracking.</p><p>- Consistent sales team touchpoints.</p><p>### Reasons for Delay</p><p>- No specific reason was provided for the target/reflection form or weekly tracker not being confirmed as complete.</p><p>- Sales team engagement appears to have dropped off because the immediate value was not always obvious.</p><p>### Repeated Missed Actions / Behaviour Patterns</p><p>- Sales team engagement has not been consistent.</p><p>- The review identified a wider pattern where actions may be intended but not always evidenced as completed.</p><p>- CRM and workflow evidence needs to show actual activity, not just intention.</p><p>### Ongoing Blockers</p><p>- Dual-system working across **Midas** and **Platform** creates process friction.</p><p>- Unclear Platform usage around source tracking and introducer adviser fields may create reporting risk.</p><p>- Lead volume and quality concerns remain unresolved.</p><p>- Paul feels output comparisons with Karen and Claire do not fully account for lead volume, lead quality, case complexity and wider support responsibilities.</p><p>### What Was Done</p><p>- The annual target position was revisited.</p><p>- Lead conversion assumptions were discussed.</p><p>- Sales team engagement was identified as a required behaviour.</p><p>- Platform usage was reviewed during the meeting, including a new lead where Paul Banyard was correctly shown as introducer adviser.</p><p>### What Was Not Done</p><p>- No completed prior action list was evidenced.</p><p>- Regular sales team meetings have not been maintained consistently.</p><p>- There is no evidence that the written and banked tracker is being completed weekly before check-ins.</p><p>- There is no evidence that Paul has fully completed the behavioural target/reflection plan.</p><p>### Risk Created by Inaction</p><p>- Lead flow may remain static.</p><p>- Referral quality may not improve.</p><p>- Management reporting may be incomplete.</p><p>- Revenue targets may not translate into specific behaviours.</p><p>- Pipeline and case ownership may weaken as appointment volume increases.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Declining / Stagnant** — formal follow-up discipline was weak, and the agreed sales team engagement activity has dropped off. This risks limiting new lead flow and weakening adviser visibility with introducers.</p><p>---</p><p>## 2. Leads Performance Review With CRM Discipline</p><p>## A. Leads – Annual / Current Reporting Position</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Paul’s targets, lead expectations, conversion performance and lead quality concerns were reviewed.</p><p>Key figures discussed:</p><p>- **406 leads**</p><p>- **21% conversion**</p><p>- **62 mortgage applications**</p><p>- **34 renewal cases**</p><p>- **40 insurance cases**</p><p>- **91 admin cases**</p><p>- Average admin fee: **£761**</p><p>- Average renewal fee: **£724**</p><p>- Average protection: **£133**</p><p>- Additional average admin fee referenced: **£249**</p><p>- Conversion figures referenced: **93%**, **50%** and **40%**, with **40%** noted as slightly lower.</p><p>- Paul’s conversion rate was also referenced at around **47%**.</p><p>- Average case value referenced at approximately **£1,800**.</p><p>- Target operating model: speak to **30 clients from 40 leads**.</p><p>- Expected conversion: **1 in 3**.</p><p>- Target outcome: around **10 new cases per month**.</p><p>- Potential income outcome from stronger conversion: around **£17,000 to £20,000+ per month**.</p><p>- Bob stated that if Paul can consistently receive and work **14 new leads per month**, while maintaining his existing conversion performance, he should exceed the target Paul has set for himself.</p><p>- Paul currently estimated he receives around **20 appointments per month**.</p><p>- Future movement discussed towards **25 to 30 appointments per month**, and eventually the business aim of **40 appointments per month**.</p><p>Paul raised concerns around lead volume and lead quality compared with peers, specifically **Karen** and **Claire**. Paul stated that his conversion from leads is “as good if not better” than theirs, but that he receives fewer leads and, in his view, lower-quality opportunities.</p><p>Bob noted that lead volumes and lead quality have been tracked for **four years**.</p><p>The lower conversion figure was linked to remortgage work, where only around **1 in 4** cases may change, compared with purchase cases where the expected average is closer to **1 in 3**.</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month / Last Year</p><p>- No direct week-on-week or month-on-month lead comparison was provided.</p><p>- Historic lead tracking over **four years** was referenced.</p><p>- Conversion was described as broadly static year on year, with only modest growth attributed mainly to the mortgage market improving rather than adviser behavioural change.</p><p>- The current year’s budget is based largely on better outcomes from a similar level of leads and opportunities as the previous year, rather than relying on significantly higher lead volume.</p><p>### Outcomes / Conversion Quality</p><p>- Paul’s conversion was recognised as strong, around **47%**.</p><p>- Business aim remains to convert at least **1 in 3** spoken-to customers.</p><p>- Remortgage conversion is expected to be lower than purchase due to client behaviour and market conditions.</p><p>- Paul’s average case value of around **£1,800** was described positively.</p><p>- Paul has generated significant business from his own activity and service levels.</p><p>### Follow-Up Effectiveness</p><p>The review challenged whether Paul is:</p><p>- Managing leads effectively from first contact to completion.</p><p>- Using all channels available to contact customers.</p><p>- Making the most of every lead.</p><p>- Following up sufficiently.</p><p>- Keeping the pipeline up to date.</p><p>- Actively working the pipeline daily and weekly.</p><p>- Identifying clients actively looking at properties.</p><p>- Assessing mortgage and protection value for active clients.</p><p>- Monitoring whether the pipeline is growing or shrinking.</p><p>### Note Quality / CRM Discipline</p><p>Expected CRM behaviours were discussed:</p><p>- Notes after each client interaction.</p><p>- Clear next steps.</p><p>- Correct workflow placement.</p><p>- Active pipeline updates.</p><p>- Passing leads back to Anna where appropriate.</p><p>- Using the correct referral system for internal property opportunities.</p><p>- Updating CRM immediately when circumstances change or blockers appear.</p><p>No specific **7+ day note gaps** were identified by client name, but weak CRM evidence and process ownership were flagged as risks.</p><p>### Source Accuracy</p><p>Platform source accuracy was discussed.</p><p>- Paul has started using **Platform** for new leads.</p><p>- A new lead was added during the review.</p><p>- The system correctly identified **Paul Banyard** as the introducer adviser.</p><p>- There remains uncertainty around where specific information is recorded in Platform, including lead source and introducer adviser fields.</p><p>### Ownership Clarity</p><p>Ownership expectations:</p><p>- **Paul Banyard:** Lead contact, workflow accuracy, client referral asking, CRM notes, pipeline ownership, protection conversations and follow-up discipline.</p><p>- **Anna:** Chase support where leads are passed back with clear context.</p><p>- **Bob / Alan / Management:** Increase quality opportunities into adviser diaries and monitor lead allocation and quality.</p><p>- **Sales teams:** Source and refer appropriate opportunities where Paul maintains visibility.</p><p>### Reporting Risks</p><p>- Dual-system working across **Midas** and **Platform** may lead to missed updates, duplicated effort or ownership confusion.</p><p>- Incorrect or incomplete Platform usage could affect reporting around source tracking and adviser ownership.</p><p>- If leads are not moved through the correct workflow or customer journey, reporting and conversion tracking may be inaccurate.</p><p>- If CRM is not updated immediately, pipeline reporting and follow-up ownership may become unclear.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Paul’s conversion rate is strong, referenced around **47%**.</p><p>- Average case value of approximately **£1,800** is strong.</p><p>- Average admin fee is strong at **£761**.</p><p>- Paul has generated a significant amount of business from his own activity and service levels.</p><p>- Paul’s own activity generated around **£140,000** of written business.</p><p>- Service levels appear to be driving repeat and referral opportunities.</p><p>- The business now has lead reports available.</p><p>- Graham has built a leads report to show:</p><p>  - How many leads are coming in.</p><p>  - What is happening with them.</p><p>  - Where they are going.</p><p>- Paul has stepped into more complex case areas, including:</p><p>  - Overseas clients.</p><p>  - Bridging.</p><p>  - Complex client circumstances such as a spouse in Uzbekistan.</p><p>- Platform has started to be used for new leads.</p><p>- A new Platform lead correctly identified Paul as introducer adviser.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Paul is not yet consistently asking every suitable client for recommendations.</p><p>- Only **£1,300** has been generated from actively asking clients for recommendations, despite broader referral business being stronger.</p><p>- Lead generation activity with sales teams has not been consistent.</p><p>- Paul feels lead allocation and quality are not comparable with Karen and Claire.</p><p>- Paul believes he is being compared on total output without sufficient allowance for:</p><p>  - Lead volume.</p><p>  - Lead quality.</p><p>  - Case complexity.</p><p>  - Additional support he gives to the wider business.</p><p>- Platform and Midas dual-system working creates friction.</p><p>- There is uncertainty around Platform fields, including lead source and introducer adviser.</p><p>- The review suggested lead follow-up may not be sufficiently robust or consistently evidenced.</p><p>- Pipeline management needs to be active on a daily and weekly basis.</p><p>- Lead volume alone will not solve performance issues without improved contact, follow-up and conversion activity.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- If Paul does not ask for recommendations consistently, referral income remains lower than potential.</p><p>- If lead status, follow-up and ownership are not consistently managed, opportunities may slip.</p><p>- If lead volume increases to **40 appointments per month**, cases may slip unless purchase, remortgage and protection processes are clearly owned.</p><p>- If Paul’s opportunity volume and quality are not addressed, he may remain behind peers in total written business despite strong conversion.</p><p>- Paul’s dissatisfaction around salary parity and bonus threshold changes may affect engagement.</p><p>- Poor CRM discipline could create unclear case status and poor management reporting.</p><p>- Dual-system working could cause missed updates or duplicated effort.</p><p>- If Paul cannot clearly identify active property-searching clients and their value, income forecasting becomes weaker.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Ask every suitable client whether they can recommend Paul to someone else. | Client referrals | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Referral income remains lower than potential. |</p><p>| Use all available contact channels: call, text and email. | Lead engagement | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Leads may go cold due to weak contact strategy. |</p><p>| If a lead does not answer, send a text and refer back to Anna for chasing where appropriate. | Lead follow-up | Paul Banyard / Anna | Ongoing | Missed contact attempts may not be followed up properly. |</p><p>| Ensure each lead is placed on the correct workflow and customer journey. | CRM / lead ownership | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Reporting becomes unreliable and cases may drift. |</p><p>| Maintain an up-to-date pipeline showing active property-searching clients, mortgage value and protection opportunity. | Pipeline / CRM | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Management cannot assess realistic opportunity or revenue risk. |</p><p>| Review whether the pipeline is growing or shrinking each week. | Pipeline health | Paul Banyard | Weekly | Lead generation issues may be identified too late. |</p><p>| Evidence follow-up actions in the CRM after each interaction. | CRM discipline | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Poor audit trail and missed follow-up risk. |</p><p>| Continue monitoring lead volume and lead quality against peers, including Karen and Claire. | Lead allocation and quality | Bob / Management | Not specified | Paul may continue to feel disadvantaged and performance comparisons may remain disputed. |</p><p>| Use lead tracking data to assess whether Paul’s opportunity volume and quality are comparable with peers. | Lead reporting | Bob / Management | Not specified | Performance comparisons may remain disputed. |</p><p>| Continue business-wide work to increase quality opportunities into adviser diaries. | Lead generation | Bob / Alan / Management | Ongoing | Advisers may not have enough opportunity to meet increased targets. |</p><p>| Use Platform as the primary system for active and early-looking clients wherever possible. | CRM / lead management | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | CRM data remains fragmented across Midas and Platform. |</p><p>| Check that self-generated leads show Paul Banyard correctly as introducer adviser. | CRM accuracy | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Reporting may not correctly attribute self-generated leads. |</p><p>| Use text messaging to improve client response rates alongside calls and emails. | Lead contact | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Lower contact rates and weaker conversion. |</p><p>| Review personal process for purchase and remortgage leads before lead volume increases further. | Lead handling process | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Capacity pressure may cause missed follow-ups or cases slipping. |</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard.</p><p>- **Admin / support:** Anna for chasing where leads are passed back.</p><p>- **Other:** Bob, Alan and management for opportunity generation, lead data and fairness monitoring.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant with improving potential** — Paul’s conversion and average case value are strong, but lead conversion behaviour has not materially moved year on year. Improvement depends on more consistent contact, CRM discipline, sales team engagement, referral asking and pipeline ownership.</p><p>---</p><p>## 3. “Where Are My Leads?”</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Paul challenged the fairness of comparisons with Karen and Claire, stating that they appear to receive more or better opportunities. Bob acknowledged that the business needs to drive more opportunities into adviser diaries.</p><p>Key points:</p><p>- The wider business focus this year will be on leads across the company.</p><p>- Management support from Alan and Bob will take place monthly to drive opportunities.</p><p>- Bob’s role will involve launching adviser activity once per month, through:</p><p>  - Individual support.</p><p>  - Group training.</p><p>  - Wider company activity.</p><p>- The aim is to increase the number and quality of opportunities.</p><p>- Paul needs to create more opportunities through proactive sales team engagement rather than waiting for lead volume to increase.</p><p>### DIP Volume Trend</p><p>No specific DIP volume trend was provided.</p><p>### Lead Quality vs Adviser Behaviour</p><p>- Paul believes lead quality and volume are not comparable with peers.</p><p>- Bob acknowledged the need to track and assess this using four years of data.</p><p>- Management focus was on controlling the controllables:</p><p>  - Lead source activity.</p><p>  - Contact quality.</p><p>  - Sales team engagement.</p><p>  - Conversion.</p><p>  - Referral asking.</p><p>  - Protection attachment.</p><p>### Engagement Frequency</p><p>- Sales team engagement has not been consistent.</p><p>- Paul needs to resume regular touchpoints with sales teams.</p><p>- Missed opportunities for regular sales team prompts included:</p><p>  - Halifax **£5,000**-related messaging.</p><p>  - Professional mortgage options.</p><p>  - NatWest professional-related opportunities.</p><p>  - Advice around affordability and buying potential.</p><p>### Stalled Cases</p><p>No specific stalled lead cases were named under this section, but the review raised concerns around cases drifting if CRM and follow-up are not maintained.</p><p>### Reporting Inconsistencies</p><p>- Lead tracking data exists, but Paul disputes fairness of output comparisons.</p><p>- Platform / Midas dual-system working may create source and ownership reporting issues.</p><p>- Informal referral routes may result in missed tracking and missed income.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Lead volume and quality are being tracked over time.</p><p>- Management recognises the need to improve opportunity flow.</p><p>- Paul’s conversion rate gives a strong basis for improvement if volume increases.</p><p>- Target client profile is clear: professional clients, often with strong incomes and good borrowing potential.</p><p>- There is a clear route to improvement through process consistency rather than relying solely on more leads.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Paul does not feel current lead allocation supports fair comparison with peers.</p><p>- Adviser-led sales team engagement has not been consistent.</p><p>- There is a risk that total written business is being used as the main comparison point without sufficient context around lead input.</p><p>- Paul’s income growth is currently too dependent on market improvement rather than adviser-led activity.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- If Paul is not front of mind with sales teams, referral volume and quality may remain static.</p><p>- If opportunity increases for all advisers equally, Paul may still remain behind peers if differences in starting point and lead quality are not addressed.</p><p>- Paul’s frustration may grow if improved performance does not lead to recognition or pay progression.</p><p>- If Paul attributes underperformance only to lead supply, behavioural gaps may not be addressed.</p><p>- Poor CRM updates could create reporting inconsistencies.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Continue business-wide work to increase quality opportunities into adviser diaries. | Lead generation | Bob / Alan / Management | Ongoing | Paul and other advisers may not have enough opportunity to meet increased targets. |</p><p>| Use lead tracking data to assess whether Paul’s opportunity volume and quality are comparable with peers. | Lead reporting | Bob / Management | Not specified | Performance comparisons may remain disputed. |</p><p>| Resume regular sales team touchpoints and provide relevant mortgage/advice updates. | Sales team engagement | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Lower referral volume and weaker lead quality. |</p><p>| Focus conversations with sales teams on professional clients and higher-quality opportunities. | Lead quality | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Diary may fill with lower-yield or more complex cases. |</p><p>| Provide sales teams with regular mortgage/advice messages to stimulate referrals. | Sales team engagement | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Paul may not remain front of mind for referrals. |</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard.</p><p>- **Other:** Bob, Alan, management and sales teams.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant with potential to improve** — the concern about lead volume and quality remains unresolved, but management has committed to increased opportunity generation. Paul also needs to rebuild consistent sales team engagement.</p><p>---</p><p>## 4. Applications in Progress</p><p>## Derek Hall / Derek</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>**Client:** Derek Hall / Derek  </p><p>**Lenders discussed:** Halifax, NatWest, Clyde sda le, Barclays  </p><p>**Current stage:** Affordability and lender assessment being reworked.  </p><p>Paul confirmed that Derek Hall is not providing credit reports. Derek has said he has checked them and that most records are registered under the address they sold at **Murray’s Brae**.</p><p>The case initially declined with **Halifax** due to credit scoring when the current address was used rather than **Murray’s Brae**. Paul reran the case, but it is still declining on affordability.</p><p>Paul stated he needs to:</p><p>- Recheck Derek Hall’s tax returns.</p><p>- Review income treatment.</p><p>- Compare what NatWest considered as income.</p><p>- Rerun affordability.</p><p>- Try a different lender.</p><p>- Use **Murray’s Brae** as the address.</p><p>Derek was also referenced as an example of a problematic or lower-yield case that was difficult from the outset and consumed adviser capacity.</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>No specific previous weekly comparison was provided, but there has been case movement:</p><p>- Initial decline with Halifax due to credit scoring.</p><p>- Rerun completed using revised address thinking.</p><p>- Case still declining on affordability.</p><p>### Delays / Missing Documents</p><p>- Derek Hall has not provided full credit reports.</p><p>- Usable income remains uncertain.</p><p>- Tax returns need to be reviewed.</p><p>- Affordability is still failing.</p><p>### SLA Breaches</p><p>No specific SLA breach was identified.</p><p>### Adviser vs Admin Responsibility</p><p>- **Paul Banyard:** Credit report request, tax return review, income assessment, affordability rerun and lender research.</p><p>- **Client:** Derek Hall to provide full credit report.</p><p>- **Admin / Case Manager:** No specific admin or case manager action was assigned.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Paul identified that the address used may be affecting credit score outcome.</p><p>- Paul is considering alternative lenders.</p><p>- Paul has recognised the need to review tax returns and income calculations.</p><p>- The case has prompted wider thinking about protecting Paul’s diary from lower-yield or problematic cases.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Credit reports have not been provided.</p><p>- The case is still not passing affordability.</p><p>- There is uncertainty around the correct income figure to use.</p><p>- Lender selection remains unresolved.</p><p>- The case has consumed adviser capacity and may distract from higher-value opportunities.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Without the credit report, Paul cannot fully evidence or diagnose the credit issue.</p><p>- Continuing without the report may lead to repeated lender declines.</p><p>- Affordability may continue to fail if income is not correctly interpreted.</p><p>- There is a risk of wasted time and unnecessary credit searches.</p><p>- Paul’s diary capacity could be reduced by lower-yield or problematic cases.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Obtain Derek Hall’s full credit report regardless of verbal confirmation. | Derek Hall | Paul Banyard / Derek Hall | Not specified | Continued uncertainty around credit profile and risk of further decline. |</p><p>| Review Derek Hall’s tax returns and confirm usable income. | Derek Hall | Paul Banyard | Not specified | Affordability may continue to fail. |</p><p>| Rerun affordability using the correct income and **Murray’s Brae** address. | Derek Hall | Paul Banyard | Not specified | Case may be assessed on inaccurate information. |</p><p>| Assess alternative lender options. | Derek Hall | Paul Banyard | Not specified | Client may remain without a viable route forward. |</p><p>| For lower-yield or lower-complexity opportunities, complete the initial conversation and assess whether the case should be passed to Sean. | Low-yield cases | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Paul’s diary capacity may be taken up by lower-value work. |</p><p>| Pass suitable cases to Sean with clear handover and required information. | Low-yield cases / Sean pipeline | Paul Banyard / Sean | Ongoing | Ownership gaps and poor client experience. |</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard.</p><p>- **Other:** Derek Hall, Sean.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant / at risk** — Paul is actively reviewing the case, but progress is blocked by missing credit reports and unresolved affordability. It also highlights a capacity risk from complex or lower-yield cases.</p><p>---</p><p>## Edinburgh Flat for Client’s Son / Castle Street</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Paul discussed a client who wants to buy a flat in **Edinburgh** for his son, who is a student. The client initially wanted a **four or five-bedroom flat** and intended to rent out the other rooms.</p><p>A property on **Castle Street** was reviewed and identified as a **five-bedroom HMO property**.</p><p>Paul explained the distinction between:</p><p>- Buying a property for the son to live in, potentially with spare rooms rented out to cover costs.</p><p>- Buying an HMO as a business opportunity.</p><p>Paul advised that the client cannot combine both routes if the property is an HMO, because an **HMO buy-to-let** would mean the son cannot live in the property.</p><p>Potential route discussed:</p><p>- Joint mortgage, sole proprietor.</p><p>- Possibly a three-bedroom flat.</p><p>- Son occupies one room.</p><p>- Other two rooms potentially rented out.</p><p>- Lender must be comfortable with the arrangement.</p><p>- Need a lender that does not require the proprietor to have minimum income because the son is a student.</p><p>Lenders considered:</p><p>- Barclays.</p><p>- Clyde sda le.</p><p>### Delays / Missing Documents</p><p>No specific missing documents were discussed.</p><p>### SLA Breaches</p><p>No SLA breach was identified.</p><p>### Adviser vs Admin Responsibility</p><p>- **Paul Banyard:** Advice clarification, lender criteria and route assessment.</p><p>- **Client:** Confirm objective and property preference.</p><p>- **Admin / Case Manager:** No specific action assigned.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Paul identified the HMO issue.</p><p>- Paul explained the difference between a residential support arrangement and an HMO business investment.</p><p>- Paul researched lender criteria and considered joint borrower / sole proprietor options.</p><p>- Paul demonstrated strong technical awareness.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- The client’s initial objective was unclear or conflicted:</p><p>  - Home for son.</p><p>  - Business opportunity.</p><p>  - HMO rental model.</p><p>- The Castle Street property does not fit the residential objective if treated as an HMO buy-to-let.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Risk of unsuitable advice if the client’s true objective is not clarified.</p><p>- Risk of wasted work if the client continues viewing unsuitable properties.</p><p>- Risk that the son’s student status limits lender availability.</p><p>- The case may fail lender criteria if the correct joint mortgage / sole proprietor route is not confirmed.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Clarify whether the client’s priority is a home for his son or an HMO business investment. | Edinburgh flat / Castle Street | Paul Banyard | Not specified | Advice route and lender selection may be unsuitable. |</p><p>| If residential route is preferred, focus search on suitable non-HMO properties, potentially around three bedrooms. | Edinburgh flat | Paul Banyard / Client | Not specified | Client may continue pursuing unsuitable properties. |</p><p>| Confirm lender criteria for joint mortgage, sole proprietor where the proprietor is a student with no minimum income requirement. | Edinburgh flat | Paul Banyard | Not specified | Case may fail lender criteria. |</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard.</p><p>- **Other:** Client.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Improving** — Paul showed good technical identification of the HMO issue and lender implications, but the case requires sharper client objective clarification before progressing.</p><p>---</p><p>## Current Month Written Cases / General Applications</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Paul stated that once he receives reports, he expects to be at **six or seven written cases** for the month to date.</p><p>Bob noted that Paul has around **two more weeks** remaining in the month to push towards the target level of approximately **17 written cases**.</p><p>No additional individual client applications, lenders, missing documents or SLA breaches were discussed.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Paul has written or expects to write **six or seven cases** so far this month.</p><p>- Bob recognised that Paul is building momentum.</p><p>- Paul is working hard.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Current written position is still below the target run rate if the aim is around **17 written cases**.</p><p>- No individual case-level detail was reviewed in this part of the discussion.</p><p>- If appointment volume rises, stronger ownership of purchase and remortgage case journeys will be required.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- If remaining cases do not progress quickly, Paul may fall short of the monthly written target.</p><p>- Without clear process ownership, increased volume could create delays.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Push current month written cases from six or seven towards the target level of around 17. | Written business | Paul Banyard | 31 May 2026 | Monthly written target may be missed. |</p><p>| Ensure purchase and remortgage case journeys are clearly managed as volume increases. | Case progression | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Cases may slip through the gaps as diary volume grows. |</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard.</p><p>- **Admin / Case Manager:** No specific actions assigned.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Improving but below target pace** — Paul has momentum, but he needs a strong final part of May 2026 to move from **six or seven written cases** towards the expected target level.</p><p>---</p><p>## 5. Protection in Progress</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Protection was reviewed as part of Paul’s annual and year-to-date performance and was identified as one of the biggest focus areas for the year.</p><p>Key figures discussed:</p><p>- Target insurance cases: **40**</p><p>- Average protection referenced in target model: **£133**</p><p>- Year-to-date average premium: **£8,893**</p><p>- Average protection case: **£182**</p><p>- Protection average previously referenced:</p><p>  - **2025:** **£1,169**</p><p>  - Current year: **£1,882**</p><p>Protection was also discussed strategically as part of the wider client journey. Bob highlighted the need to make sure the quality of meetings is strong and that insurance contact is good.</p><p>No individual protection clients were reviewed.</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month / Last Year</p><p>- No week-on-week or month-on-month protection comparison was provided.</p><p>- A comparison was made to previous protection average performance:</p><p>  - **2025:** **£1,169**</p><p>  - Current year: **£1,882**</p><p>- The review noted a significant drop-off in one area of protection performance and asked Paul to consider how to maintain or slightly grow protection output rather than allow it to reduce.</p><p>- Protection has historically been below target.</p><p>### Cases Without Protection Discussion</p><p>No specific client cases without protection discussion were named, but the review identified that protection must be embedded into every purchase and remortgage opportunity.</p><p>### Missed Cross-Sell Opportunities</p><p>Potential missed opportunities exist across:</p><p>- Purchase cases.</p><p>- Remortgage cases.</p><p>- First-time buyer opportunities.</p><p>- Returning client reviews.</p><p>- Cases where mortgage work increases but protection attachment remains weak.</p><p>### Clients Not Contacted</p><p>No specific uncontacted protection clients were identified.</p><p>### Delays in Recommendation</p><p>No specific protection recommendation delay was identified.</p><p>### Weak Effort Trends</p><p>- Protection has not yet been evidenced as a consistent part of every client journey.</p><p>- There was no evidence discussed of a structured protection follow-up process.</p><p>- Protection performance requires closer focus to prevent decline.</p><p>- Paul needs to “squeeze a little bit more” from protection opportunities.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Protection is included in the target model.</p><p>- Paul has year-to-date protection data available.</p><p>- The average protection case figure of **£182** was noted.</p><p>- Protection has been clearly identified as a priority.</p><p>- Remortgage and first-time buyer opportunities provide natural protection conversation points.</p><p>- Stronger client contact and better case management should improve protection outcomes.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Protection performance requires closer focus.</p><p>- Protection has historically been below target.</p><p>- No client-level protection activity was reviewed.</p><p>- No structured protection follow-up process was evidenced.</p><p>- Protection is not yet positioned as a consistent part of every client journey.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Missed cross-sell opportunities.</p><p>- Lost income if protection is not discussed early and consistently.</p><p>- Lower bonus outcome if mortgage cases increase but protection attachment remains weak.</p><p>- Client protection gaps.</p><p>- Mortgage conversion alone may not be enough to hit total revenue expectations.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Build protection discussion into every purchase and remortgage opportunity. | Protection | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Missed cross-sell opportunities and lower client protection levels. |</p><p>| Define specific actions to deliver **40 insurance cases** this year. | Protection target | Paul Banyard | Not specified | Protection target may be missed. |</p><p>| Ensure protection activity is documented within the relevant client journey. | Protection / CRM | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Weak evidence of advice and missed follow-up opportunities. |</p><p>| Ensure protection discussions are embedded into purchase and remortgage client journeys. | Protection process | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Missed protection opportunities and reduced client value. |</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant / needs improvement** — protection remains a known weakness and has been identified as a major area requiring change this year. No evidence was reviewed showing improved protection activity or outcomes.</p><p>---</p><p>## 6. Written Business – This Month / Year-to-Date</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Paul’s written business and revenue targets were discussed in the context of annual performance and the current month.</p><p>Key figures discussed:</p><p>- Revenue completed / done: **£145,000**</p><p>- Target being set or considered: **£125,000** referenced in relation to expenses.</p><p>- Desired target level shown on spreadsheet: around **£170,000**</p><p>- Paul’s own hard work generated approximately **£140,000** of written business.</p><p>- New business from people looking to speak to Paul via Keep in Financial Services was **£33,000** when client referrals were removed.</p><p>- Actively asked-for client recommendations generated only **£1,300**.</p><p>- Current month expected written cases once reports are received: **six or seven**</p><p>- Current month target level discussed: around **17 written cases**</p><p>- Remaining time in month: around two weeks from the review date of **20 May 2026**, giving until **31 May 2026**.</p><p>### Written vs Same Month Last Year</p><p>No specific same-month-last-year written business figure was provided.</p><p>### Pipeline Expected to Write</p><p>- Paul expects reports to move him to **six or seven written cases**.</p><p>- Additional opportunities need to progress before **31 May 2026** to move towards the **17 case** target.</p><p>### Likelihood of Target Being Hit</p><p>- At the point of review, Paul was below target pace.</p><p>- Momentum is positive, but there is a clear risk of missing the monthly written target without a strong final part of May 2026.</p><p>### Adviser Confidence Level</p><p>Paul indicated that once reports are received he expects to reach **six or seven written cases**, but no explicit confidence rating was provided for hitting **17**.</p><p>### Reasons for Performance Difference</p><p>- Lead volume and quality concerns.</p><p>- Protection and referral opportunities not fully maximised.</p><p>- Client recommendation asking is materially underused.</p><p>- Potential capacity drain from lower-yield or complex cases.</p><p>- Process and CRM discipline need to strengthen as opportunity volume increases.</p><p>### Learning Observations</p><p>- Paul’s underlying conversion and average case value are strong.</p><p>- Growth to **£170,000** requires more proactive behaviour, not just maintaining existing service levels.</p><p>- Referral asking needs to become standard.</p><p>- Sales team engagement needs to become consistent.</p><p>- Case prioritisation matters, especially where lower-yield cases consume adviser capacity.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Paul has produced strong revenue from his own activity.</p><p>- Paul’s service appears to generate repeat and referred business.</p><p>- Admin fee has materially improved and should be maintained.</p><p>- Bob stated that Paul has momentum and is working hard.</p><p>- Paul’s conversion and average case value were described positively.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Referral asking is underused.</p><p>- Move towards **£170,000** requires stronger process consistency.</p><p>- Written business growth depends on proactive behaviour.</p><p>- Current month written position is not yet at the required run rate.</p><p>- There is reliance on further cases progressing during the remaining part of May 2026.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- If Paul does not change behaviour, he may not achieve the larger revenue jump required.</p><p>- Passive referrals may not be sufficient to bridge the gap.</p><p>- Weak tracking of written and banked business could delay corrective action.</p><p>- Shortfall risk if Paul does not move from **six or seven written cases** towards approximately **17**.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Maintain the improved admin fee level while increasing volume. | Written business | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Revenue growth may be diluted by lower average fees. |</p><p>| Track written business weekly in the performance form. | Written business tracking | Paul Banyard | Weekly | Shortfalls may not be identified early. |</p><p>| Increase actively requested client referrals beyond the current **£1,300** level. | Client referrals | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Paul may miss controllable self-generated income. |</p><p>| Progress current opportunities into written business before month-end. | Written business | Paul Banyard | 31 May 2026 | Written performance may fall short of target level. |</p><p>| Push current month written cases from six or seven towards the target level of around 17. | Written business | Paul Banyard | 31 May 2026 | Monthly written target may be missed. |</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Improving but at risk** — Paul has a strong base and positive momentum, but the current month requires significant progress before **31 May 2026** to move towards the expected written target.</p><p>---</p><p>## 7. Banked Business – This Month / Pipeline</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Banked business and pipeline position were discussed.</p><p>Key figures:</p><p>- Desired banked target referenced: **£180,000**</p><p>- Pipeline / already banked position: just under **£50,000**</p><p>- Q1 pipeline / pot so far: **£31,000**</p><p>- Monthly bank threshold increasing from **£18,750** to **£21,750**</p><p>- Increase in threshold: **£3,000**</p><p>- Paul noted this equates to around **£600** less bonus opportunity before he starts earning, based on **20% of £3,000**</p><p>- Wider business current performance referenced at approximately **£650,000**</p><p>- Wider business goal around **£1,000,000**</p><p>- Business shortfall around **£350,000**</p><p>Bob stated that the business needs Paul to focus on banking this year.</p><p>### Banked vs Last Year</p><p>No specific banked comparison against last year was provided.</p><p>### Pipeline Still to Complete</p><p>- Just under **£50,000** already in the bank / pipeline.</p><p>- Q1 position of **£31,000**.</p><p>- Written cases need to progress into banked business.</p><p>- Current pipeline gives Paul a strong start but needs replenishing.</p><p>### Slippage Risk</p><p>- Written cases may not bank as expected.</p><p>- Pipeline could shrink if not actively replenished.</p><p>- Overconfidence from the early pipeline could reduce urgency on new business generation.</p><p>- Increased banking threshold creates pressure before bonus is earned.</p><p>### Revenue Exposure</p><p>- Personal exposure: increased threshold from **£18,750** to **£21,750**.</p><p>- Business-level exposure: approximately **£350,000** short of the required minimum level.</p><p>- Engagement risk due to Paul’s concerns around salary and bonus structure.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Paul has a strong opening pipeline position.</p><p>- Q1 already has **£31,000** in the pot.</p><p>- Existing pipeline gives Paul a “foot up” into the new financial year.</p><p>- Paul’s average case value and conversion indicate stronger banking is achievable if lead volume improves.</p><p>- Bob stated Paul now has momentum.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Paul is dissatisfied with the increased banking threshold.</p><p>- Paul has not had a pay rise for **three years**.</p><p>- Paul believes peers Karen and Claire have higher basic salaries.</p><p>- Increased target creates pressure before bonus is earned.</p><p>- Paul must avoid relying on existing pipeline instead of continuing to write new business.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Pipeline may shrink if not actively replenished.</p><p>- Banked business may slip if written cases do not complete as expected.</p><p>- Increased bank target without improved lead volume may create performance and morale risk.</p><p>- Business-level shortfall creates pressure on all advisers.</p><p>- Paul’s engagement may be affected by perceived unfairness.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Continue writing new business despite having just under **£50,000** already in the bank / pipeline. | Banked business / pipeline | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Future pipeline may weaken. |</p><p>| Monitor Q1 pipeline and maintain momentum from the current **£31,000** position. | Q1 pipeline | Paul Banyard | Weekly | Early advantage may be lost. |</p><p>| Maintain focus on banked income and improve conversion of opportunities into completed cases. | Banked business | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Increased bank threshold may not be achieved. |</p><p>| Management to continue reviewing opportunity flow so Paul has sufficient volume to support the increased banking target. | Opportunity generation | Bob / Management | Ongoing | Banking shortfall may continue despite strong conversion. |</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard.</p><p>- **Other:** Bob / Management.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Improving but under pressure** — Paul has a strong starting position, but increased banking targets and salary concerns create morale and revenue risk.</p><p>---</p><p>## 8. Renewal / Remortgage Income</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Renewal and remortgage opportunities were reviewed.</p><p>Key figures:</p><p>- Target renewal cases: **34**</p><p>- Paul’s total remortgage / renewal opportunities this year: **57**</p><p>- Expected conversion target: **60%**</p><p>- Broader business remortgage performance discussed around **50%**</p><p>- Wider average referenced: **4%**</p><p>- Business-wide remortgage pool: **320 clients**</p><p>- Around **11 clients** may potentially have applications submitted in December 2026.</p><p>- The previous December was quiet.</p><p>- December 2026 is expected to be significantly busier.</p><p>- Some remortgage applications may be submitted up to six months early where lender criteria allows.</p><p>- NatWest was discussed as an example of a lender with a six-month mortgage offer window.</p><p>The review noted that not all remortgage opportunities will convert. The business will track:</p><p>- Whether reviews are being booked.</p><p>- Whether booked reviews are progressing.</p><p>- Whether clients are disengaging.</p><p>- Why conversion is below or above the **60%** expectation.</p><p>### Clients in Pipeline</p><p>- Paul has **57** remortgage opportunities in scope.</p><p>- Business-wide pipeline includes **320 clients**.</p><p>- Around **11** potential December 2026 applications.</p><p>### Reviews Booked</p><p>No specific number of reviews booked was provided.</p><p>### Insurance Gaps</p><p>- Remortgage reviews create protection opportunities.</p><p>- Protection must be included in remortgage conversations.</p><p>- Missed remortgage reviews may mean missed protection and referral opportunities.</p><p>### Clients Still to Contact</p><p>- The **57** remortgage opportunities need to be proactively contacted and tracked.</p><p>- No client-level contact list was reviewed.</p><p>### Forecast vs Expectation</p><p>- Expected conversion target is **60%**.</p><p>- Current broader business performance around **50%**.</p><p>- Wider average referenced as **4%**.</p><p>- Paul’s opportunity base is strong, but execution is required.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Paul has **57** potential opportunities.</p><p>- Returning client performance is materially stronger than the wider average.</p><p>- Business-wide opportunity base of **320 clients** is strong.</p><p>- December 2026 could generate significant application activity.</p><p>- Early submission may help protect clients from foreseeable harm if rates move adversely.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- It is not yet clear whether all opportunities are being booked in.</p><p>- It is not yet clear where fall-out occurs:</p><p>  - Not booked.</p><p>  - Booked but not progressing.</p><p>  - Not suitable.</p><p>  - Client disengaged.</p><p>- No specific contact plan for the **57** clients was evidenced.</p><p>- No CRM activity, client contact notes or follow-up schedule was reviewed.</p><p>- Larger pipeline will require stronger planning and client management.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Paul may under-convert the **57** opportunities if reviews are not booked promptly.</p><p>- December 2026 may become capacity-heavy if the pipeline is not worked early.</p><p>- Clients may suffer foreseeable harm if suitable options are not secured early where appropriate.</p><p>- Rate volatility creates timing risk.</p><p>- Dual-system working between Midas and Platform may make remortgage tracking harder.</p><p>- Poor tracking may hide the reason for non-conversion.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Track all **57** remortgage opportunities through to outcome. | Remortgage pipeline | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Conversion cannot be accurately measured. |</p><p>| Aim for **60%** conversion and identify reasons for any shortfall. | Remortgage conversion | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Renewal income may fall below expectation. |</p><p>| Ensure remortgage reviews are booked and followed up. | Renewal reviews | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Clients may disengage or be lost. |</p><p>| Proactively contact the **57** potential clients available this year. | Renewal / remortgage pipeline | Paul Banyard | Ongoing through 2026 | Remortgage and referral income may be missed. |</p><p>| Build and maintain a clear contact plan for remortgage clients. | Remortgage pipeline | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Clients may disengage or be lost to competitors. |</p><p>| Plan remortgage contact strategy early for the 57-case pipeline. | Remortgages | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | December 2026 capacity pressure and missed opportunities. |</p><p>| Consider six-month early submission options where suitable, including NatWest cases. | Remortgages | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Clients may miss suitable rates or suffer foreseeable harm. |</p><p>| Revisit clients after initial rate lock-in where market review is appropriate. | Remortgages | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Clients may not benefit from improved rates if market changes. |</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Improving opportunity, execution required** — the remortgage pipeline is materially stronger than the previous year, but income depends on early planning, proactive contact, CRM discipline and conversion tracking.</p><p>---</p><p>## 9. Remortgage and Internal Property Referral Opportunities</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>The review highlighted that some of Paul’s **57** remortgage opportunities may involve clients selling their house, buying another property or letting a current property.</p><p>These should create internal referral opportunities.</p><p>The referral system can be used for:</p><p>- Sales referrals.</p><p>- Lettings referrals.</p><p>- Referring a client selling a property to any branch.</p><p>Paul was advised that if he is only sending emails, he may be losing out financially. Instead, he should use the referral system on **Neighborhoods**.</p><p>Bonus payment is made when the company gets paid on that business.</p><p>In another part of the review, referral opportunities were described as needing to be passed to the correct branch or contact. Where branch territory is unclear, Paul should check before sending the referral.</p><p>### Clients Chased</p><p>No individual remortgage clients were confirmed as chased.</p><p>### Clients Disengaged</p><p>No individual disengaged clients were named.</p><p>### Re-Engagement Strategy</p><p>- Proactively contact all **57** remortgage clients.</p><p>- Build and maintain a contact plan.</p><p>- Use CRM updates immediately when circumstances change.</p><p>- Identify sale, purchase or lettings needs.</p><p>- Log internal referrals correctly through **Neighborhoods**.</p><p>- Check branch territory if unclear.</p><p>### Weak Follow-Up Trends</p><p>- The discussion did not evidence a specific contact plan for the **57** clients.</p><p>- There was no review of CRM notes or follow-up schedule.</p><p>- Informal email referrals may be used instead of the correct referral system.</p><p>- Referral income may be missed if clients are not identified and passed across correctly.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- There is a clear internal referral process available.</p><p>- Paul has a significant pool of clients where property sale or purchase activity may arise.</p><p>- Bonus opportunity is immediate once the business pays.</p><p>- Business-wide remortgage volume is strong.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Paul may not be using the Neighborhoods referral system consistently.</p><p>- If referrals are sent informally by email, financial benefit may be missed.</p><p>- There may be a lack of habit or understanding around the internal referral process.</p><p>- Incorrect branch referral could slow down client contact unless checked first.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Missed internal referral income.</p><p>- Missed estate agency or lettings opportunities for the wider business.</p><p>- Poor tracking of referral outcomes.</p><p>- Referral may go to the wrong branch and client contact may be delayed.</p><p>- Paul may not get paid directly on some remortgage-related cases where a referral could still generate income.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Use the Neighborhoods referral system for all clients selling, buying or requiring lettings support. | Internal referrals | Paul Banyard | Immediate / ongoing | Paul may lose referral bonus income. |</p><p>| Review the **57** remortgage opportunities for potential sales or lettings referrals. | Remortgage / referrals | Paul Banyard | Not specified | Internal referral opportunities may be missed. |</p><p>| Stop relying on informal email referrals where the system should be used. | Referral process | Paul Banyard | Immediate / ongoing | Referrals may not be tracked or paid correctly. |</p><p>| Identify clients with properties to sell and pass referral opportunities to the correct branch or contact. | Property sale referrals | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Referral income may be lost. |</p><p>| Where branch territory is unclear, check first before sending the referral. | Referral process | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Referral may go to the wrong branch and client contact may be delayed. |</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard.</p><p>- **Other:** Branch contacts / property teams.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Declining risk if not corrected** — there is clear income potential, but Paul may miss value if referrals are not logged through the correct system or routed correctly.</p><p>---</p><p>## 10. Projected Income</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>Projected income was reviewed using annual target assumptions, current performance, pipeline, lead conversion and business-level banking requirements.</p><p>Key figures:</p><p>- Revenue completed / done: **£145,000**</p><p>- Desired spreadsheet target: around **£170,000**</p><p>- Banked target discussed: **£180,000**</p><p>- Just under **£50,000** already in the bank / pipeline.</p><p>- Q1 pot: **£31,000**</p><p>- Required purchase cases: **62**</p><p>- Required renewal cases: **34**</p><p>- Required insurance cases: **40**</p><p>- Lead expectation: **406 leads**</p><p>- Conversion: **21%**</p><p>- Conversion rate also referenced around **47%**</p><p>- Average case value around **£1,800**</p><p>- Target contact level: **30 conversations from 40 leads**</p><p>- Target conversion: **1 in 3**, producing around **10 new cases per month**</p><p>- Potential monthly income outcome: **£17,000 to £20,000+**</p><p>- Wider business goal: around **£1,000,000**</p><p>- Current wider business performance: approximately **£650,000**</p><p>- Wider business shortfall: approximately **£350,000**</p><p>- If wider team banks **£1,000,000**, profit margin may be around **£200,000 to £250,000**</p><p>- Current year profit margin discussed as around **£10,000**</p><p>### Pipeline vs Budget</p><p>- Paul has a strong start with just under **£50,000** already supporting the year.</p><p>- Q1 pipeline is healthy at **£31,000**.</p><p>- Desired target is around **£170,000–£180,000**.</p><p>- Growth depends on converting lead volume, remortgage opportunities, protection and referrals.</p><p>### Shortfall</p><p>- Personal target risk exists between current performance and the higher target range.</p><p>- Business-level shortfall is around **£350,000**.</p><p>- Paul’s increased personal banking threshold creates further pressure.</p><p>### Plan to Close Gap</p><p>- Convert existing lead volume more effectively.</p><p>- Speak to **30 out of 40 leads**.</p><p>- Convert **1 in 3** spoken-to clients.</p><p>- Deliver around **10 new cases per month**.</p><p>- Build protection into every mortgage and remortgage conversation.</p><p>- Work the **57** remortgage opportunities.</p><p>- Use internal referral opportunities.</p><p>- Maintain average case value and admin fee.</p><p>- Prioritise professional clients and stronger borrowing cases where appropriate.</p><p>- Avoid allowing lower-yield cases to consume too much capacity.</p><p>### Behaviour Impacting Results</p><p>Positive behaviours:</p><p>- Strong technical case handling.</p><p>- Strong average case value.</p><p>- Strong service-led business generation.</p><p>- Willingness to handle complex cases.</p><p>Behavioural risks:</p><p>- Inconsistent sales team engagement.</p><p>- Weak active referral asking.</p><p>- Protection not fully embedded.</p><p>- CRM and workflow discipline not consistently evidenced.</p><p>- Potential over-focus on lower-yield or problematic cases.</p><p>- Frustration around pay, bonus thresholds and peer comparison.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Strong start with pipeline and Q1 pot.</p><p>- Paul has strong service-led business generation.</p><p>- Admin fee performance is strong.</p><p>- Conversion and average case value provide a strong income base.</p><p>- Bob believes that if opportunity volume improves, Paul can write and bank more than in previous years.</p><p>- Remortgage and referral opportunities provide additional income routes.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Growth to the higher target requires process improvement.</p><p>- Referral asking is materially below potential.</p><p>- Protection activity needs strengthening.</p><p>- Pipeline must be actively managed rather than passively monitored.</p><p>- Income growth is too dependent on market movement rather than adviser behaviour.</p><p>- Inconsistent engagement with sales teams limits control over lead flow.</p><p>- Current business income is short of required level.</p><p>- Lead volume remains a concern.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Revenue may fall short of **£170,000–£180,000** if Paul maintains prior behaviour only.</p><p>- Missed client referrals and internal referrals could reduce controllable income.</p><p>- Lead conversion below expected **1 in 3** could undermine targets.</p><p>- Paul may not meet increased personal bank target if lead volume and quality do not improve.</p><p>- Process weaknesses could become a blocker as appointment volume rises.</p><p>- If protection does not increase, overall income and bonus potential may remain limited.</p><p>- If Paul focuses heavily on lower-yield cases, capacity for higher-income opportunities will reduce.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Build a clear plan to deliver **62 purchase cases**, **34 renewal cases** and **40 insurance cases**. | Annual income plan | Paul Banyard | Not specified | Income target may not be achieved. |</p><p>| Review performance weekly against written, banked, purchase, remortgage and insurance activity. | Projected income | Paul Banyard | Weekly | Shortfall may not be identified early enough. |</p><p>| Drive increased appointments while maintaining conversion and case quality. | Projected income | Bob / Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Income gap may remain. |</p><p>| Strengthen ownership of purchase and remortgage processes before volume increases further. | Operating process | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Higher volume may create missed follow-ups and lost income. |</p><p>| Focus on converting existing lead volume more effectively rather than waiting for more leads. | Income / lead conversion | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Income remains static despite available opportunities. |</p><p>| Prioritise professional clients and stronger borrowing cases where appropriate. | Income / case quality | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Lower average case value and weaker income growth. |</p><p>| Use protection and admin fee opportunities consistently alongside mortgage business. | Income / protection | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Reduced bonus and lower total income. |</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard.</p><p>- **Other:** Bob / Management.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant with upside / improving but exposed** — Paul’s performance indicators are positive, but projected income depends on behavioural improvement, lead quality, banking conversion, remortgage execution, protection attachment and process capacity.</p><p>---</p><p>## 11. CRM Discipline and Case Ownership</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>CRM and workflow discipline were discussed in relation to leads, pipeline management, Platform usage, Midas legacy clients and referral tracking.</p><p>Expected behaviours include:</p><p>- Notes after client interactions.</p><p>- Clear next steps.</p><p>- Correct workflow placement.</p><p>- Active pipeline updates.</p><p>- Passing leads back to Anna where appropriate.</p><p>- Using Platform for active and early-looking new clients.</p><p>- Continuing Midas management for legacy clients where required.</p><p>- Checking lead source and introducer adviser fields.</p><p>- Using the Neighborhoods referral system for internal opportunities.</p><p>- Updating CRM immediately where circumstances change or blockers arise.</p><p>### Poor CRM Usage</p><p>Specific poor CRM themes flagged:</p><p>- Potential lack of evidence of follow-up.</p><p>- Unclear workflow status.</p><p>- Dual-system friction between Midas and Platform.</p><p>- Possible informal email referrals instead of logged Neighborhoods referrals.</p><p>- Lack of reviewed contact plan for the **57** remortgage clients.</p><p>- Weak evidence of protection activity being documented.</p><p>- Risk that leads are not placed on the correct customer journey.</p><p>No named **7+ day note gaps** were identified.</p><p>### Ownership Gaps</p><p>Potential gaps include:</p><p>- Paul / Anna handover if non-responsive leads are not passed back with clear context.</p><p>- Paul / Sean handover if lower-yield cases are transferred without full information.</p><p>- Branch referral routing if territory is unclear.</p><p>- Platform source tracking if introducer adviser is not checked.</p><p>- Remortgage pipeline ownership if the **57** clients are not tracked through to outcome.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Reports and spreadsheets are available.</p><p>- Paul has access to the data needed to track performance.</p><p>- Support is available from Anna for chasing.</p><p>- Management support is available for lead focus.</p><p>- Platform correctly identified Paul as introducer adviser on a new lead reviewed during the meeting.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Follow-up commitments must be evidenced and completed.</p><p>- Opportunities may slip if actions are not tracked.</p><p>- Informal processes may create ownership and reporting gaps.</p><p>- CRM usage across Midas and Platform is not yet fully clear.</p><p>- Protection documentation is not evidenced as consistent.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Poor notes and unclear workflow status may create reporting risk.</p><p>- Missed follow-ups may cause leads to disengage.</p><p>- Ownership may be unclear between Paul, Anna, Sean and other teams if handovers are not logged.</p><p>- Reporting becomes inaccurate if CRM is not updated immediately.</p><p>- Referral income may be missed if referrals are not logged in the correct system.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Add clear CRM notes and next steps after each customer interaction. | CRM discipline | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Poor audit trail and missed follow-up risk. |</p><p>| Pass appropriate chasing activity to Anna with clear context. | Lead handling | Paul Banyard / Anna | Ongoing | Leads may not be chased effectively. |</p><p>| Use the correct workflow and referral systems rather than informal routes. | CRM / referrals | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Reporting inaccuracies and missed income. |</p><p>| Update CRM immediately when circumstances change or blockers appear. | CRM discipline | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Reporting becomes inaccurate and follow-up gaps may occur. |</p><p>| Use Platform as the main system for new active and early-looking clients. | CRM / Platform | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | CRM data remains fragmented. |</p><p>| Check lead source and introducer adviser details when adding self-generated leads. | CRM accuracy | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Self-generated leads may not be attributed correctly. |</p><p>### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Paul Banyard.</p><p>- **Admin:** Anna where chasing is passed over.</p><p>- **Other:** Sean where appropriate cases are handed over.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Stagnant** — systems are available, but Paul needs to evidence consistent ownership through CRM updates, workflow accuracy, logged follow-ups, documented protection activity and correct referral routing.</p><p>---</p><p>## 12. Overall Performance Diagnosis</p><p>### Adviser Strengths This Week</p><p>- Strong conversion performance, referenced around **47%**.</p><p>- Strong average case value, referenced around **£1,800**.</p><p>- Strong average admin fee at **£761**.</p><p>- Strong service-led business base, with around **£140,000** generated from Paul’s own activity.</p><p>- Positive pipeline position, with just under **£50,000** already in place and **£31,000** in Q1.</p><p>- Paul is working hard and has improved performance through **2025/2026**.</p><p>- Paul has stepped into more complex case areas, including overseas clients, bridging and complex client circumstances.</p><p>- Paul demonstrated strong technical awareness in the Edinburgh / Castle Street HMO discussion.</p><p>- Paul is considering lender criteria carefully on complex cases including Derek Hall and the Edinburgh student-son flat.</p><p>- Paul is proactive in challenging fairness around lead quality, peer comparison and remuneration.</p><p>### Main Risks</p><p>- Derek Hall remains blocked by missing credit report and affordability issues.</p><p>- Edinburgh flat case could follow the wrong route unless the client’s objective is clarified.</p><p>- Protection remains a key underperformance risk.</p><p>- Active referral asking is significantly underused, with only **£1,300** generated from actively requested recommendations.</p><p>- Internal referral income may be missed if Neighborhoods is not used.</p><p>- Lead quality and volume concerns remain unresolved.</p><p>- Paul may not reach the written target of around **17 cases** by **31 May 2026**.</p><p>- Increased bank threshold from **£18,750** to **£21,750** adds pressure.</p><p>- Paul’s dissatisfaction over salary stagnation and bonus threshold increase creates engagement risk.</p><p>- If lead volume increases without stronger process, Paul may hit capacity and cases may slip.</p><p>- Remortgage and referral opportunities may be missed without structured follow-up.</p><p>- Dual-system working across Midas and Platform creates CRM/reporting risk.</p><p>- December 2026 remortgage workload may create capacity pressure if not planned early.</p><p>### Behaviour Patterns</p><p>- Strong technical case handling where complexity is identified.</p><p>- Good service levels generating organic repeat and referred business.</p><p>- Less evidence of structured, proactive referral asking.</p><p>- Need for more consistent documented follow-up and pipeline ownership.</p><p>- Potential reliance on informal routes, such as email referrals, rather than using correct systems.</p><p>- Paul is highly engaged and willing to challenge perceived unfairness.</p><p>- Paul appears motivated by fairness, recognition and comparison with peers.</p><p>- There is a risk of frustration if improved activity and additional complexity are not recognised commercially.</p><p>- Income growth is currently too dependent on market movement rather than adviser-led behavioural change.</p><p>- Sales team engagement has not been maintained consistently.</p><p>### Accountability Concerns</p><p>- Paul needs to convert targets into specific behaviours.</p><p>- Weekly written and banked tracking must be completed before check-ins.</p><p>- CRM and workflow updates need to evidence activity, not intention.</p><p>- Referral opportunities must be owned and logged correctly.</p><p>- Protection activity needs to be documented.</p><p>- Management needs to ensure lead allocation and quality are measured transparently against peers if output comparisons continue.</p><p>- Lower-yield cases need clear triage and handover where suitable.</p><p>### Where Management Support Is Required</p><p>- Monthly lead focus with Bob, Alan and management.</p><p>- Increasing quality opportunities into Paul’s diary.</p><p>- Reviewing lead volume and quality against Karen and Claire.</p><p>- Supporting Paul with process design before higher appointment volume creates capacity issues.</p><p>- Managing concerns around salary parity, bonus threshold changes and recognition.</p><p>- Guidance on correct use of the Neighborhoods referral system where needed.</p><p>- Continued support on lead reporting and conversion tracking.</p><p>- Support from Anna for chasing where Paul provides clear context.</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>**Improving foundation, but execution, morale and capacity risks remain** — Paul has strong underlying business quality, conversion, case value and pipeline. The performance uplift depends on disciplined CRM ownership, sales team engagement, referral asking, protection focus, remortgage planning and management support around opportunity fairness and remuneration concerns.</p><p>---</p><p>## 13. Master Action List</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>|---|---|---:|---:|---|</p><p>| Complete the target and reflection form, including what worked well, what did not work well, challenges and planned changes. | Annual performance planning | Paul Banyard | Not specified | Targets may not translate into behaviour change. |</p><p>| Fill in written and banked business weekly before check-ins. | Revenue tracking | Paul Banyard | Weekly | Management cannot track progress accurately. |</p><p>| Re-establish regular meetings and touchpoints with sales teams. | Sales team engagement | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Lead flow and adviser profile remain static. |</p><p>| Provide sales teams with regular useful mortgage/advice messages to stimulate referrals. | Sales team engagement | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Paul may not remain front of mind for referrals. |</p><p>| Focus conversations with sales teams on professional clients and higher-quality opportunities. | Lead quality | Paul Banyard | Ongoing | Diary may fill with lower-yield or more complex cases. |</p><p>| Continue monitoring lead volume and lead quality against peers, including Karen and Claire. | Lead allocation and quality | Bob / Management | Not specified | Paul may continue to feel disadvantaged and performance comparisons may remain disputed. |</p><p>| Use lead tracking data to assess whether Paul’s opportunity volume and quality are comparable with peers. | Lead reporting </p><p>[Notes truncated for file size — full notes available in Plaud]</p><p>Full transcript</p><p>Speaker 1: So when we&#x27;re adding it into the items. That&#x27;s sitting on the Barclays portal.</p><p>Speaker 1: And, so I Think.</p><p>Speaker 1: So what we need to do is</p><p>Speaker 1: Property details, so you don&#x27;t have to fill out the whole thing. You just need to note what&#x27;s changed. If you want anything like me, just give me a quick WhatsApp. I&#x27;ll get it sorted. So we&#x27;re going to be on together on Friday. I think you need to start with that, but I think once you get used to it, you can start understanding what&#x27;s behind things a bit more interesting. But they kind of get much more practice later on. These are my interests. But I think it&#x27;s good if they have a basis.</p><p>Speaker 1: But that&#x27;s the thing. I was saying not knowing. Comes really