: I get lower quality. Up, I can&#x27;t remember how you would word that.</p><p>Paul Banyard: It just feels like we get to the end of next year. Paul&#x27;s written one hundred and seventy eight K, butum, Leads have ramped up for Claire and Karen as well, and they&#x27;ve delivered two hundred. So you are still a little bit behind them, Paul. So you can forget about getting an equitable salary to your colleagues.</p><p>Speaker 2: See, that&#x27;s my problem right now.</p><p>Speaker 4: So that has tried to look at the positive here. Our leads have been tracked for four years. The quality of those leads has been tracked for four years. The plan for this year is from me, and Alan told me that I&#x27;m going to talk about it.</p><p>Speaker 2: My job will be to launch all of the devices once a month, either setting up certain individuals, doing group training, or whatever it might be across the whole of the company. The plan is that we want to drive more opportunities. If you can drive more opportunities, you will have less opportunity in your diary. You&#x27;ll have a month full of practice cases. I&#x27;ll give you fifteen cases a month instead of maybe fifty or five folks in your team like that. Five. But effectively could double that number. That&#x27;s what you want. I appreciate you have done a lot, and I know you have really stepped up this year and really focused on some of the things you wanted to do this year. However, sadly the company hasn&#x27;t been in a position to support it this year. But next year we can really get your.</p><p>Speaker 2: I can do what it needs to be. Our argument was, as you&#x27;re only in your peers and actually you desire to be the next year&#x27;s peers, we need to get you for that next year. But we also need athletes to deliver and teams to deliver. But we need you to keep doing what you&#x27;re doing because actually, what you&#x27;re doing is you work really hard and I look forward and I mean, know it has a bit of a negative, but