u with this remortgage for all. Fifty seven cases. Last year you had a really quiet December. You&#x27;re probably going to have a really busy December this year. There were eleven clients that have got applications submitted in December potentially. So in December was predominantly the client actually. You&#x27;re probably going to be submitting some of those cases. And then you do some of the ones in December, you might be doing a six month deal out. You&#x27;ve got Nat West with a six month mortgage offer to get. Sometimes their rates are all over the place at the moment, and we should be advising customers to tie in now where they can.</p><p>Speaker 2: And then we visit in three months to see where we&#x27;re at because I can wait three months. Actually, it costs them foreseeable harm. She wishes I&#x27;ve done something three months ago. I think the best thing to do is how you bring it back into this. That is how your plan for this year is small tweaks to get you to deliver what you want to deliver. Your biggest focus this year is going to be the insurance aspect. You had a target of if you delivered just a quarter of what the target set for, you need to deliver that this year. So remortgage books are going to grow. Purchase book, roughly speaking, because of the average case, size is slightly smaller, but you&#x27;re really going to be focusing on adding these first time purchasers and remortgages. That&#x27;s where the small gains are. It&#x27;s not rocket science. Really isn&#x27;t, but we need to get more over the lead side.</p><p>Speaker 2: Coming in, need to get to. Speaking to thirty, convert them ten cases a month and that&#x27;s new cases not just cases. If you do that, the remortgage picks your bonus, your insurance cases are going to rise anyway, and your admin fees naturally around price too, so you will have more bonus.</p><p>Paul Banyard: I&#x27;d say this year&#x27;s going to be a challenge because you&#x27;re working off two systems. You&#x27;re