y dealing with</p><p>Speaker 1: I think it&#x27;s.</p><p>Speaker 2: The spike.</p><p>Speaker 1: London. Here&#x27;s something. So our average income.</p><p>Speaker 2: There you go. Our average income for clients is around one hundred k. Dealing with professional customers, underground. So that&#x27;s the kind of these are our average income. And that&#x27;s the main income for the main driver. We&#x27;re dealing with professional people. These are the people that we&#x27;re writing for mortgages for right now. We&#x27;re not dealing with that low end. We&#x27;re dealing with professional clients with good incomes, and that&#x27;s what we need them to drive more of. More of these opportunities here, three hundred, four hundred, five hundred k&#x27;s. We all want millions, but we&#x27;ll never get the millions they might appear. But if you have more of those in your diary, you will write more business and that has got to be our focus.</p><p>Paul Banyard: What&#x27;s your thoughts on low income or low yield cases? For example, we may not have the luxury of saying it&#x27;s beneath what we would do. Paraphrasing, Edinburgh says they don&#x27;t sell properties for any less than two fifty two eighty. They&#x27;ve got the luxury of doing that because they&#x27;re turning a profit obviously. Cases like Derek were a shit show from day one.</p><p>Speaker 2: And future as we go on.</p><p>Paul Banyard: Just what your attitude is to those sorts of shitty cases.</p><p>Speaker 2: We do them, we charge our fees. What I would do is actually pass them to you and have your diary open in the future for something like going into care.</p><p>Paul Banyard: So do the initial call and go, this is for Sean.</p><p>Speaker 2: That&#x27;s the size of the market. It&#x27;s quite a big one. Sean needs that experience. He&#x27;s happy. He&#x27;s quite happy with that. He&#x27;s going to manage that lead. You need to move new bits of that, the little type, but actually it&#x27;s about him building his own pipeline and building his own book. Actually, in a nice way, you are probably better dealing with an Edinburgh sales team than trying to get mortgage. What would you rather have? Let me ask that. So there is absolutely have that nice conversation. We can definitely help here. No problem at all. Go and get the X Y Z.</p><p>Speaker 2: He&#x27;s picking up that experience. He&#x27;s building his knowledge. He&#x27;s slowly growing that role about business things as well. And in fact, it&#x27;s the same question. I had to clear this action about all this low hanging fruit stuff. I am like, &quot;Well, that&#x27;s perfect for Sean,&quot; and she agrees with that again. She wants to give the cases of Sean on which not having an effort call.</p><p>Speaker 4: Cool.</p><p>Speaker 2: Cool? We&#x27;ll let you crack on. I am late for Craig. Goodbye!</p><p>Paul Banyard: Cheers Bob!</p><p>Speaker 2: She&#x27;s had that too now.</p></body></html>